\description{
This function provides a comprehensive statistical analysis for tumor growth data using
different statistical approaches. It supports multiple methods including linear mixed-effects
models, generalized additive mixed models, and area under the curve analysis.
}
\details{
This function offers several statistical approaches for analyzing tumor growth data. The choice of model
depends on the experimental design, growth patterns, and research questions:
\enumerate{
\item Linear mixed-effects models (model_type="lme4") are suitable for most tumor growth experiments and
account for the correlation structure of repeated measurements on the same subjects.
The default formula is log(Volume) ~ Day * Treatment + (Day | ID): per-animal random
slopes, falling back to uncorrelated slopes and then to a random intercept only when a
fit fails (CODE_REVIEW.md R20.5).
\item Area under the curve analysis (model_type="auc") compares arms by the area under each arm's
fitted curve (a mixed model of log volume with a natural spline in time per arm and random
slopes) from the first study day to the last day on which every arm is evaluable. Arms are
compared by the ratio of their AUCs, with 95\% intervals and Wald p-values from draws of the
model. Each animal's own trapezoidal AUC is kept in auc_analysis$individual for description
only: it covers that animal's follow-up, so it is not comparable across arms with different
attrition (CODE_REVIEW.md R20.4).
}

The function automatically handles:
\itemize{
\item Missing data patterns using appropriate methods
\item Checking for and addressing cage effects
\item Transformations to normalize growth curves
\item Polynomial terms for non-linear growth patterns
\item Diagnostic checks of model assumptions
\item Growth rate analysis for each treatment group
\item Cage effect analysis and collinearity testing
\item A fallback to simpler random effects when the requested structure cannot be fitted
\item Treatment effect estimation with proper reference group handling
}
}
